Because $\triangle ACD$ is isosceles with $AC=CD$, $\measuredangle CAD=\measuredangle CDA=\theta$, where $\theta$ is some angle measure. Because $\angle CDA$ and $\angle ADB$ form a straight angle, $\measuredangle ADB = 180^{\circ}-\theta$. Thus, because the interior angles of a triangle add to $180^{\circ}$, $\measuredangle BAD + \measuredangle ABC = \theta$, so $\measuredangle ABC = \theta - \measuredangle BAD$. Notice that $\measuredangle CAB = \theta + \measuredangle BAD$. With all of this information and recalling that, from the problem, $\measuredangle CAB - \measuredangle ABC = 30^{\circ}$, we see that:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\measuredangle CAB - \measuredangle ABC &= 30^{\circ} \\
\theta + \measuredangle BAD - (\theta - \measuredangle BAD) &= 30^{\circ} \\
2\measuredangle BAD &= 30^{\circ} \\
\measuredangle BAD &= 15^{\circ}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus, our answer is $\boxed{\textbf{(E) }15^{\circ}}$.
